; 09-qed.kurt

; `qed` closes the most recent `proof` block (08-proof.kurt).  It takes
; everything derived inside the block, tries once more to derive the shown
; goal, and -- if that succeeds -- adds the *goal* (not the intermediate
; steps) to the theory, at the level where it was `show`n.

bool A, B

use A implies B
use A

show B
proof
    A          ; re-deriving something we already had is fine
qed

B              ; `B` is now available outside the block

; If the goal cannot be derived, `qed` raises a `ProofError` instead of
; closing the block -- try commenting out `use A implies B` above and rerun
; this file to see it fail.

; `qed` must match the indentation of the `show`/`proof` it closes exactly --
; dedenting is how Kurt knows which (and how many nested) blocks to close; a
; single `qed` can close several nested blocks at once this way.

; `qed` is actually optional: dedenting alone already closes a `proof` block
; the same way, `qed` or not -- it's there to document where a proof ends,
; and to double-check you're really closing a `proof` and not some other
; kind of block. So the block above could just as well have been:
;
;     show B "no qed needed"
;     proof
;         A
;
; both forms behave identically; use whichever reads better to you.
